{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788135968,"nonce":"j/fgQCB70d2pLMKYfqbBDtT+Enk=","message":"WORKER REPORT — opus worker, orchestrator session 5, BORGES. Branch\n`spec/1.5-substrate-remediation` (shared with 1.5 per the dispatch), isolated\nworktree. Code written and compiled; **the game was never launched**, so every\nin-game acceptance bullet below is reported as **NOT demonstrated**.\n\n1.7's commits, separate from 1.5's as required:\n\n- `105521b` 1.7: advance halts with reason `dialog` under a force-pausing modal\n- `a53e694` 1.7/1.5: fixture steps and a status surface the acceptance can be\n  driven from\n\n\nTwo detectors, one halt, no window ever touched.\n\n- **The frame step polls `WindowStack.WindowsForcePause` once per TICK**, not\n  per frame — because a `LetterWithTimeout` opens itself from\n  `LetterStackTick`, which is inside the very `DoSingleTick` the loop is\n  executing. The `WindowStack` is cached once per FrameStep, so a window-free\n  advance pays a null check plus a loop over an empty list per tick and nothing\n  else.\n- **A Harmony postfix on `WindowStack.Add`** journals a first-class `dialog`\n  event for any force-pausing window opened while `Current.ProgramState ==\n  Playing`. `Add` calls `PreOpen()` before the postfix runs, so a window that\n  sets `forcePause` in `PreOpen` is caught too. Because `Journal.Emit` fires\n  `TimeDriver.Notice` synchronously on the emitting thread, this halts one tick\n  earlier than the poll would; the poll is the backstop.\n- **Reason set is now** `ticks | timeout | interrupted | letter | threat |\n  alert | event | red_error | dialog`.\n- **Identification.** `halted_on.windows[]` carries `type` (short class name —\n  the assertable identifier), `type_full` (so a modded window is\n  distinguishable from a vanilla namesake), `title` when `optionalTitle` is\n  set, and `layer`. Plus `letters` — up to 10 labels from\n  `LetterStack.LettersListForReading` — because the window type says a dialog\n  is up and the letter stack says *which decision is owed*, which is what 3.5\n  actually needs to route on.\n- **The standing invariant** is `force_pause_windows` on the advance result,\n  computed LIVE at Finish rather than copied from the halt, so a window that\n  went up during the final frame appears even when the halt reason was\n  something else. Absent means the stack is clear.\n- **`status.json` and the `status` verb** now carry `forcePause` in the same\n  payload shape whenever a modal is up, absent otherwise. That is how the\n  orchestrator sees \"left with the modal up rather than ticking beneath it\"\n  without reading the screen. The snapshot allocates nothing when the stack is\n  clear.\n- `JOURNAL.md` documents the `dialog` type and a section on why `advance`\n  halts on it.\n\nVerified against the decompiled 1.6 source on THIS bench (content, not\noffsets, per the dispatch note): `TickManager.DoSingleTick` →\n`Find.LetterStack.LetterStackTick()` (TickManager.cs:463 here, same as the\nLinux tree) → `OpenAutomaticLetters()` (LetterStack.cs:154), which opens with\n`if (Find.WindowStack.WindowsForcePause) return;`;\n`LetterWithTimeout.ShouldAutomaticallyOpenLetter =\u003e LastTickBeforeTimeout`;\n`ChoiceLetter.OpenLetter` stacks a `Dialog_NodeTreeWithFactionInfo` whose\n`Dialog_NodeTree` ctor sets `forcePause = true`. Counted 54 files setting\n`forcePause = true` — all genuine modals, none a window that is persistently\nopen during unattended play, so the halt cannot fire spuriously.\n\nOne thing worth knowing: `LudeonTK/Dialog_Debug` and\n`Dialog_DebugOptionLister` are on that list. Opening the dev debug menu on a\nwatched bench WILL halt the next advance with `reason:\"dialog\"`. That is\ncorrect behaviour, not a bug, but it will surprise someone.\n\n\n\nThe defect is entirely mechanical: `OpenAutomaticLetters` early-returns on\n`WindowsForcePause` and on nothing else. A non-pausing window does not touch\nthe letter queue, and vanilla runs at Ultrafast underneath them all day. The\nforce-pause flag is not a proxy for \"something is up\" — it IS the predicate\nthat breaks the letter queue, so halting on it is exact rather than heuristic.\n\nHalting on any window would also make `advance` unusable rather than\nconservative: `Find.WindowStack` routinely holds non-pausing windows during\nordinary play (main tab windows, the inspect pane, float menus, the dev log\nwindow that `Log.Error` opens whenever devMode is on — and devMode is on for\nevery fixture in this repo). An advance that halted on those would halt\nconstantly and for no reason connected to any defect.\n\nThey are also not silently ignored: `force_pause_windows` reports the pausing\nset, and if a later spec wants full window visibility, `status.forcePause` is\nthe place to widen — one field, one shape, no change to the halt contract.\n\n\nThe spec says \"Do NOT close windows\" and I have not: nothing in 1.7 closes,\nfocuses or reorders a window. Deciding what a dialog MEANS — which `DiaOption`\nto take, whether a trade offer should be accepted — is 3.5's job and cannot be\ndone correctly by a verb that only knows how to make a window go away.\n\n**But this leaves a genuine wedge, and M1 is a ten-day unattended run.** Once\nan advance halts with `dialog`, no shipped verb can clear the stack, so every\nsubsequent advance halts immediately at `ticks_elapsed:0` and the run is stuck\nuntil a human intervenes. That is strictly better than the current silent\ncorruption — a stuck run is visible, an expired letter queue is not — but it\nis not a state M1 can survive, and **3.5 is therefore on M1's critical path,\nnot a nice-to-have.** Worth saying out loud on the muster.\n\nThe acceptance needs a clear stack for its second bullet, so the escape hatch\nexists as a **fixture step, dev-gated, where no player verb can reach it**:\n`journal-selftest {steps:[\"dialogs-clear\"]}`. Disclosed below.\n\n\nThere is no honest \"plough on\". For as long as a force-pausing window is up,\n`OpenAutomaticLetters` returns at its first line, so a suppressed halt does not\nmean \"keep going with a dialog in the corner\" — it means \"keep going with the\nletter queue switched off\". Every trade caravan, quest offer and timed threat\nfor the rest of the session expires unseen. A flag whose honest documentation\nis \"this silently destroys game content\" should not exist; the caller who wants\nit wants 3.5's dialog routing instead, and giving them a flag now would remove\nthe pressure to build it.\n\nThe narrower question — \"may I keep ticking to reach a tick target, accepting\nthe loss?\" — has no legitimate user either. `rwtest` wants determinism, and\nthis path is the opposite of it. If one ever appears, it should be\n`allow_letter_loss:true` with that name, not `halt_on_dialog:false`, so the\ncost is in the argument rather than in a comment.\n\n\nAuthorised by the dispatch with a disclosure duty. All keep the `Prefs.DevMode`\ngate, all journal a `dev` event for provenance, all are superseded by 3.1.\n\n- **`timeout-letter`** — 1.7's mechanism on the real vanilla path, not a stub.\n  `LetterMaker.MakeLetter` over `LetterDefOf.NeutralEvent` yields a\n  `StandardLetter` (a `ChoiceLetter`, hence a `LetterWithTimeout`);\n  `StartTimeout(N)` arms `disappearAtTick`; the letter then opens itself from\n  `LetterStackTick`. The result reports the exact tick it will open at, so the\n  orchestrator can size the advance rather than guess. Args:\n  `timeout_ticks_letter` (default 120, 2..600000), `letter_tag`.\n- **`dialogs`** — read-only report of the force-pause stack, same payload shape\n  as `status.json` and the advance result, so one assertion holds against all\n  three.\n- **`dialogs-clear`** — the escape hatch above. Closes force-pausing windows\n  via `WindowStack.TryRemove(window, doCloseSound:false)` and, by default,\n  drops the fixture letters. **The letter drop is not tidiness:** a\n  `LetterWithTimeout` still inside its last tick re-opens the SAME dialog on\n  the very next tick, which would read as a poisoned queue when nothing is\n  wrong. `drop_fixture_letters:false` turns it off. It matches only letters\n  whose label starts with `[AutoRimmer] timeout letter`, so it can never touch\n  a letter the colony cares about.\n\n\n`$R` is the protocol root (`status.json`'s directory; `rwa status` prints it,\nor `\u003csave-data\u003e/AutoRimmer`). devMode must be on. Start from a loaded colony\nwith the game paused.\n\n\n```bash\nR=\"$HOME/.config/unity3d/Ludeon Studios/RimWorld by Ludeon Studios/AutoRimmer\"   # adjust\nw(){ printf '%s' \"$2\" \u003e \"$R/commands/.$1.tmp\"; mv \"$R/commands/.$1.tmp\" \"$R/commands/$1.json\"; }\nr(){ for i in $(seq 1 ${2:-120}); do [ -f \"$R/results/$1.json\" ] \u0026\u0026 { cat \"$R/results/$1.json\"; return; }; sleep 1; done; echo \"TIMEOUT $1\"; }\n\nw d1-arm '{\"id\":\"d1-arm\",\"op\":\"journal-selftest\",\"args\":{\"steps\":[\"timeout-letter\"],\"timeout_ticks_letter\":300}}'\nr d1-arm | jq '.data.timeout_letter'\nw d1-adv '{\"id\":\"d1-adv\",\"op\":\"advance\",\"args\":{\"ticks\":20000,\"max_tps\":300}}'\nr d1-adv | jq '{reason:.data.reason, ticks:.data.ticks_elapsed, halted:.data.halted_on, fp:.data.force_pause_windows}'\njq -r '.forcePause' \"$R/status.json\"\n```\n\nProves it when: `reason == \"dialog\"`; `ticks_elapsed` is ~299 and far short of\n20000; `halted_on.windows[0].type == \"Dialog_NodeTreeWithFactionInfo\"`;\n`halted_on.letters` contains `[AutoRimmer] timeout letter`;\n`force_pause_windows` is present (the modal is still up at return); and\n`status.json`'s `forcePause` block is present and stays present — the game is\nsitting under the modal, not ticking beneath it. Cross-check `tick` in two\n`status.json` reads ten seconds apart: it must not move.\n\n**NOT demonstrated.**\n\n\nRun bullet 1 first, then:\n\n```bash\nw d2-clear '{\"id\":\"d2-clear\",\"op\":\"journal-selftest\",\"args\":{\"steps\":[\"dialogs-clear\"]}}'\nr d2-clear | jq '.data.dialogs_cleared'\njq -r 'has(\"forcePause\")' \"$R/status.json\"          # must be false now\nw d2-arm '{\"id\":\"d2-arm\",\"op\":\"journal-selftest\",\"args\":{\"steps\":[\"timeout-letter\"],\"timeout_ticks_letter\":300,\"letter_tag\":\"second\"}}'\nr d2-arm | jq '.data.timeout_letter'\nw d2-adv '{\"id\":\"d2-adv\",\"op\":\"advance\",\"args\":{\"ticks\":20000,\"max_tps\":300}}'\nr d2-adv | jq '{reason:.data.reason, ticks:.data.ticks_elapsed, letters:.data.halted_on.letters}'\ngrep -c '\"type\":\"dialog\"' \"$R/journal/\"*.ndjson\n```\n\nProves it when: `dialogs_cleared.closed` names the window and\n`still_force_paused` is false; the SECOND advance also halts with\n`reason:\"dialog\"` at ~299 ticks with `halted_on.letters` containing\n`[AutoRimmer] timeout letter second`; and the journal holds TWO `dialog`\nevents. A poisoned queue would show the second advance running the full 20000\nticks with `reason:\"ticks\"` and no second `dialog` event — which is exactly\nwhat the unfixed build does, and is the regression this issue exists for.\n\n**NOT demonstrated.**\n\n\n```bash\nw d3-clear '{\"id\":\"d3-clear\",\"op\":\"journal-selftest\",\"args\":{\"steps\":[\"dialogs-clear\"]}}'; r d3-clear \u003e/dev/null\nfor i in 1 2 3; do\n  w d3-$i \"{\\\"id\\\":\\\"d3-$i\\\",\\\"op\\\":\\\"advance\\\",\\\"args\\\":{\\\"ticks\\\":50000,\\\"max_tps\\\":1000}}\"\n  r d3-$i 600 | jq -c '{reason:.data.reason, ticks:.data.ticks_elapsed, wall:.data.wall_seconds, tps:.data.avg_tps, fp:(.data.force_pause_windows!=null)}'\ndone\n```\n\nProves it when: all three report `reason:\"ticks\"`, `ticks_elapsed:50000`, no\n`force_pause_windows`, and `avg_tps` within noise of the FINDINGS §6 baseline\nfor this bench (~1392 tps at the 30 fps cap). Compare against the same run on\n`main` if you want the before/after in one place. The guard is a cached null\ncheck plus `WindowsForcePause` over an empty list per tick, so any measurable\nregression here means something else is wrong.\n\n**NOT demonstrated.**\n\n\n```bash\njq -c 'select(.type==\"red_error\")' \"$R/journal/\"*.ndjson\n```\n\nProves it when: empty across all of the above. Note the `error`/`error-at`\nfixture steps deliberately produce red errors for 1.5's acceptance — run 1.7's\nbullets in a session that does not use them, or filter on the known text\n`[AutoRimmer] selftest-induced red error (deliberate)`.\n\n**NOT demonstrated.**\n\n\n- **3.5 is on M1's critical path**, per resolution 2. A halted-on-dialog run\n  cannot resume itself.\n- The dev debug menus force-pause. Expect `reason:\"dialog\"` if you open one\n  while watching.\n- `Prefs.AutomaticPauseMode` can make vanilla call `TickManager.Pause()` on\n  letter arrival; harmless here, since the advance loop never reads\n  `TickManager.Paused`.","files":null}]}